= Introduction 

== Definitions

*General* Define $[k] := {1,...,k}$. Denote $cal(P)(X)$ the set of subset of $X$ and $|X|$ the cardinal of $X$. For $A,B$ two sets, denote $A^B$ the sets of function from $B$ to $A$ and denote $A union.sq B$ the disjoint union of $A$ and $B$. Denote $cal(P)_k (X)$ or $binom(X,k)$ the set of subset of cardinal $k$. Denote by $binom(X,n_1,...,n_k)$ the set of partitions of the form $union.big.sq_(i) A_i = X$ with $|A_i| = n_i$, and by $binom(n,n_1,...,n_k) = |binom([n],n_1,...,n_k)|$. It is well known that $
binom(n,n_1,...,n_k) = (n!)/(n_1 ! n_2 ! ... n_k !)
$

*Graphs* A graph is a $G = (V,E)$ with $E subset.eq cal(P)_2(V)$, and an oriented graph has $E subset.eq V^2$. Both can have multi-graph variants, where every edge $e$ is associated to an arity $\#(e) in NN^*$. For $T = (V_T,E_T,r)$ a rooted tree, denote $T(x)$ the subtree tree rooted at $x$, and by $|T|$ the number of nodes in $T$. For $G$ a graph, denote $deg v$ the degree of $v$ and if $G$ is oriented, denote $deg^+ v$ the outgoing degree and $deg^- v$ the ingoing degree. If $G$ is a multi-graph, the degree is the sum of the arities. For $S subset.eq V_G$, denote by $G[S]$ the (multi)graph induced on $S$. If $G$ is a multi-graph, denote $overline(G)$ the same graph where all arities is $1$, i.e. the regular graph behind $G$. A _path_ is a sequence of vertices $chevron v_1,...,v_k chevron.r$ with $forall i < k, \#(v_i,v_(i+1))>=1$ and a _co-path_ is a sequence of vertices $chevron v_1,...,v_k chevron.r$ with $forall i < k, \#(v_(i+1),v_i)>=1$

*Grammars* For $cal(G)$ a grammar, denote $cal(G) chevron chevron.r$ the set of contexes over $cal(G)$. For $C$ a context denote $C chevron N chevron.r$ the context where the hole has been replaced with $N$.

*$lambda$-calculus* Denote $Lambda$ the set of all lambda terms and $arr_beta$ the beta reduction. Denote $I := (lambda x.x)$ the identity $lambda$-term. All lambda term will be considered up to $alpha$-equivalence. We therefore have that $arr_beta$ is the set of the form $ C chevron.l (lambda x. e ) e' chevron.r arr_beta C chevron.l e[x <- e'] chevron.r $

*Identity Terms* Let $Lambda_I$ be the set of identity-terms generated by the grammar $e, e' ::= (lambda x. x) | e space e'$. It is a subset of all the lambda terms. One can see that $Lambda_I$ is closed under $arr_beta$, with $arr_beta$ being in this case of the form 
$ C chevron.l I e chevron.r arr_beta C chevron.l e chevron.r $

*Rewrite systems* A rewrite system $R = (V,arr)$ is an oriented multi-graph with $E_G = (->)$. Nodes $v in V$ such that $deg^+ v = 0$ are called _final nodes_ or _normal forms_, and nodes such that $deg^- v = 0$ are call initial nodes. A path $(p_1,...,p_n)$ in $G$ is called a reduction sequence and is noted $p_1 -> p_2 -> ... -> p_n$ or $p arr^n p_n$. We denote by $arr^*$ the transitive reflexive closure of $->$. For $R$ a rewrite system, we have $->$.

Two systems $(V_1,E_1)$ and $(V_2,E_2)$ are said to be isomorphic if there exists a bijection $Phi : V_1 --> V_2$ such that $(x,y) in E_1 <=> (Phi(x),Phi(y)) in E_2$ and that $Phi$ preserve the arity. We will denote this by $(V_1,E_1) equiv (V_2,E_2)$


== Rewrite system of interest (TO-REDO)

We denote $ORTrees$ the set of rooted tree were children are ordered from left to right (min to max or 1 to $n(T)$). Such a tree can be represented using a finite $V_T subset.eq NN^*^omega$ closed under prefixes and such that if $u beta in V_T$ then $forall alpha < beta, u alpha in V_T$. In that case, $max {alpha in NN| u alpha in V_T}$ is the number of children of the subtree $u^(-1) T$. For $T$ a tree we denote $n_T$ the number of children of the root. We can also write $N(x_1,...,x_n)$ the tree with children $x_1,...,x_n$.

