= Counting

For $T$ a tree, denote $N(T)$ the number of way to reduce it into a leaf, so that $N(T)$ is the number of path from $T$ to the empty tree, counting the multi-edges. Such a path is called a _reduction sequence_ and is represented by a sequence $chevron x_1,...,x_n chevron.r$ of vertices of $T$ such than $x_i$ is a leaf in $T[{x_(i+1),...,x_n}]$. $x_i$ represent morally the $i$-th node to be removed.

*Theorem* For all trees $T$, we have $ N(T) = (|T|!)/(product_(x in V_T) |T(x)|) $


*Proof* By induction on $|T|$. If $|T| = 1$, then there is one way, that is to remove the only node / leaf. Let us consider a tree $T$ with $|T| = n+1$. Denote the root $r$ and the direct children of the root as $x_1,...,x_d$. Let's us fist count the number of path of $T$ in function of $(N(T(x_i)))_i$:

For all $i<= d$, let $p^((i))$ be a reduction sequence of $A_i$. Remark that for all $i in [d]$ we have $|p^((i))| = |A_i|$. For every partition $X_1 union.sq ... union.sq X_d$ of $[ n ]$ such that $|X_i| = |A_i|$ we can create the reduction sequence $p = chevron p_1,...,p_n chevron.r$ defined by induction by letting $p_(i)$ be the next element in $p^((j))$ such that $i in X_j$ (unique because it is a partition). By appending the root to $p$, we obtain a reduction sequence of $T$.

So for every $d$-uplet of reduction sequence of each children and each partionning of $[n]$ respecting the number of edges of each subtree, we can obtain a reduction sequence. This is a bijective mapping. Let $p$ be a reduction sequence. It ends with $r$, so let's ignore this node. We can extract the subsequence $p^((i))$ from splitting $p_i$ in function of which nodes occurs in which $T(x_i)$. This gives us both the partition and the set of subsequences.

We therefore arrive at this formula :

$ N(A) 
&= binom(n-1,|A_1|,...,|A_k|) product_(i=1)^k F(A_i) 
$

Then for every $i <= d$, $|T(x_i)| <= n$ so we can apply the induction hypothesis to every $T(x_i)$. Denote $n_i = |T(x_i)|$, we have :
$ 
N(T) 
&= binom(n-1,n_1,...,n_d) product_(i=1)^k F(T(x_i)) \
&= (n-1)!/(n_ 1!...n_k !) product_(i=1)^k (n_i !)/(product_(x in T(x_i)) |T(x_i)|) \
&= (n-1)!/(product_(i=1)^k product_(x in T(x_i)) |T(x_i)|)  \
&= (n-1)!/(product_(x in V_T \\{r}) |T(x_i)|) &union.big.sq_(i<= d) T(x_i) "is a partition of" V_T \\{r}\
&= n!/(product_(x in T) |T(x_i)|) 
$


*Long chain*
