= Dyslexic trees

Denote by $T equiv T$ the fact that two left-to-right rooted trees are the same (aka same dyck words, aka same identity-term).

For a fixed $P subset.eq NN$, we define a tree transformation $rho_P^i (T)$ with $i$ the height by induction  :
$
rho_P^i(l) &= l " for " l " a leaf" \
rho_P^i (N(x_0,...,x_n))
  &= N(rho_P^(i+1) (x_n),...,rho_P^(i+1) (x_0)) " if " i in P \
rho_P^i (N(x_0,...,x_n))
  &= N(rho_P^(i+1) (x_0),...,rho_P^(i+1) (x_n)) " if " i in.not P
$

We define the equivalence relation $T prop T' <==> exists P subset.eq NN, rho^0_P (T) = T'$. It is an equivalence relation as taking the xor (symetric difference) of two $P$ would work, and the same $P$ work for $T' prop T$. For $T prop T'$, there is an isomorphism as rooted unordered tree $phi_T^P : V_T <--> V_(T')$. We will omit $P$ and $T$ when it's clear from context. $rho$ is an operation on TREES while $phi$ is the operation on vertices of a fixed tree. We can see that $T equiv T' => rho_P (T) equiv rho_P (T')$ as it is a function defined on rooted ordered trees. 

*Lemma* $rho_P^i$ is an involution.

*Proof* By induction on the tree. The leaf case is trivial. For $T = N(x_0,...,x_n)$, if $i in P$, then
$
rho_P^i (T) = N(rho_P^(i+1)(x_n),...,rho_P^(i+1)(x_0)).
$
Applying $rho_P^i$ again reverses the order once more, and by induction each $rho_P^(i+1)(rho_P^(i+1)(x_j)) = x_j$. Hence we get back $N(x_0,...,x_n)$. The case $i in.not P$ is identical but without the reversal of the node itself. Therefore $rho_P^i compose rho_P^i = id$.

*Subtrees* If $S subset.eq V_T$ is connected and contains the root, then $T[S]$ is an induced subtree. Denote $cal(S)(T)$ the sets of such connected subset containing the root. Morally, we removes iteratively all the leafs outside of $S$. Notice that $S |-> T[S]$ is not always injective.

*Naturality Lemma* Let $T$ be an ordered tree and $S in cal(S)(T)$, then $ forall P subset.eq NN, forall i in NN, space rho^i_P (T)[ phi_P^T (S) ] equiv rho_P^i (T[S]). $

*Proof* By induction on $T$. If $T = l$ is a leaf, then necessarily $S = V_T$, hence $rho_P^i (T)[phi_P^T (S)] = l = rho_P^i (T[S]).$

Suppose now $T = N(x_0,...,x_n).$ For every $0 <= i <= n$, let $S_i = S inter V_(x_i).$ Since $S$ is connected and contains the root, every $S_i$ is either empty or belongs to $cal(S)(x_i)$. Assume first that $0 in.not P$. Then $rho_P^0(T) = N( rho_P^1(x_0),..., rho_P^1(x_n))$.

Restricting to the vertices of $phi_P^T (S)$ keeps exactly the children corresponding to non-empty $S_i$. Therefore
$
rho_P^0 (T)[phi_P^T (S)]
=
N( (rho_P^1(x_i)[phi_P^(x_i)(S_i)] )_i ).
$

By induction hypothesis, we have for each $i$ the fact that $rho_P^1(x_i)[phi_P^(x_i)(S_i)] equiv rho_P^1(x_i [S_i]).$ Hence, since the root is in $S$ and not in $P$
$
rho_P^0(T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv N(( rho_P^1(x_i [S_i]) )_i) equiv rho_P^0 (N((x_i [S_i])_i)) equiv rho_P^0(T[S]).
$

The case $0 in P$ is identical, except that both sides reverse the order of the children simultaneously: we have
$
rho_P^0 (T)[phi_P^T (S)]
=
N( (rho_P^1(x_(n-i))[phi_P^(x_(n-i))(S_i)] )_i ).
$
and therefore
$
rho_P^0(T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv N(( rho_P^1(x_(n-i) [S_(n-i)]) )_i) equiv rho_P^0 (N((x_i [S_i])_i)) equiv rho_P^0(T[S]).
$
*Corollary* For all $S,S' in cal(S)(T)$ such than $T[S] equiv T[S']$, we have
$ forall P subset.eq NN, space rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S')] $
This is because since $T[S] equiv T[S']$ then $rho^0_P (T[S]) equiv rho^0_P (T[S'])$ so we have by the naturality $ rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S)] equiv rho_P^0 (T[S]) equiv rho_P^0 (T[S']) equiv rho^0_P (T)[phi_P^T (S')]. $

*Theorem* If $T prop T'$, then $DAG(T) equiv DAG(T')$.

*Proof* Suppose $rho^0_P (T) = T'$. Define the isomorphism :
$
Phi : DAG(T) &<--> &DAG(T') \
T[S] &mapsto.long &rho^0_P (T[S])
$

This is well-defined by the naturality lemma. Since $rho_P^0$ is an involution, the same construction applied from
$T'$ to $T$ gives the inverse map, hence $Phi$ is bijective.

Now we show that $Phi$ preserves edges. Since $Phi$ is involutive, we just need to proove that if $A_1 -> A_2$ is an edge in $DAG(T)$ with multiplicity $m$, then $Phi(A_1) -> Phi(1_2)$ is an edge in $DAG(T')$ with multiplicity $m' >= m$.

Suppose that there is an edge $A_1 -> A_2$ in $DAG(T)$ with multiplicity $m$. Then there is $m$ ways to write $A_1 equiv T[S],A_2 equiv T[S']$ where $S' = S - {l}$ for some leaf $l$ of $T[S]$. Denote $M$ the set of couples $(S,l)$ such than $A_1 equiv T[S]$ and $A_2 equiv T[S \\ {l}]$.

Let $(S,l) in M$. Because $phi$ is a rooted tree isomorphism, $phi(l)$ is a leaf of $T'[phi(S)]$. Also, $phi(S \\ {l}) = phi(S) - {phi(l)}$. Therefore there is an edge $T'[phi(S_1)] -> T'[phi(S_2)]$. It remains to check multiplicities. 

Consider the map $gamma :(S,l) |-> (phi(S), phi(l))$. It is injective as $phi$ is. So edge multiplicities are preserved.

Therefore $DAG(T) equiv DAG(T'). square$

