= Categorical aspect


*Subtrees* If $S subset.eq V_T$ is connected and contains the root, then $T[S]$ is an induced subtree. Denote $cal(S)(T)$ the sets of such connected subset containing the root. Morally, we removes iteratively all the leafs outside of $S$. Notice that $S |-> T[S]$ is not always injective.

Let's define the category $ORTrees$ whose objects are Ordered (left-to-right) Rooted Trees. We'll denote the root as $r$. We define the morphisms $ORTrees(T,T') = {S in cal(S)(T) | T[S] equiv T'}$. We say that a morphism $S in ORTrees(T,T')$ is elementary if $|V_T - S| = 1$ (aka we only remove a single node, a leaf)

*Remarks*
- There is a terminal object, the tree with only a root, with only morphisms $S = {r}$

*Theorem* Let $F : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ be an equivalence of categories. Then
$
  F(T) equiv T' => DAG(T) equiv DAG(T').
$

*Remark* The previous case was just $F(T) = rho_P (T)$ and $F(T ->_S T') = rho_P (T) -->_(phi_P (S)) rho_P (T')$ 

*Proof.* Since $F$ is an equivalence of categories, it is fully faithful and essentially
surjective. In particular, for all trees $A,B$, the map
$
  F_(A,B) : ORTrees(A,B) --> ORTrees(F(A),F(B))
$
is a bijection. We can define $Phi : DAG(T) --> DAG(F(T))$ as $Phi(T') = F(T')$ : it is indeed a application into $"DAG"(T)$ as for all $A in DAG(T)$, we have a morphism $T -->_S A in ORTrees(T,A)$, so a morphism $F(T) -->_(F(S)) F(A) in ORTrees(F(T),F(A))$, so $A in DAG(F(T))$.

Conversely, because $F$ is an equivalence, it has a quasi-inverse $G$. Applying
the same argument to $G$, every vertex of $DAG(T')$ is equivalent to some
$F(A)$ with $A$ a vertex of $DAG(T)$. Hence $Phi$ is a bijection on vertices,
up to $equiv$.

It remains to prove that $Phi$ preserves edges and their multiplicities. This is because a morphism $f : A --> B$ removes exactly one leaf if and only if $f$ is
non-invertible and irreducible (A simple cardinality argument on $S$ in $A -->_S B$ for both ways). Since equivalences of categories preserve and reflect isomorphisms, factorisations,
and irreducibility, every distinct edge of $DAG(T)$ is sent to a distinct edge $F(A) --> F(B)$ of $DAG(T')$. Thus edge multiplicities are preserved.

So $Phi : A |-> F(A)$ induces an isomorphism of directed multigraphs and we have
$
  DAG(T) equiv DAG(T').
$
$square$

*Questions!*
- Are all equivalences of categories $F : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ of the form $rho_P$ ?
- If $Psi : ORTrees --> ORTrees$ preserve the DAGs, is it necessary an equivalence of categories? Or can we lighten the hypothesis?

If both questions are yes, we have an iff with $scripts(prop)_P$. If the first is no, we should be able to get a bigger equivalence relation than $scripts(prop)_P$. If the second is false (it probably is), can we get a bigger equivalence using a $Psi$ that isn't an equivalence of categories?

